{"causes":["The rise of nationalism in the 19th century created a need for a neutral language to bridge cultural divides.","The increasing global communication and travel in the early 20th century prompted the desire for an international auxiliary language.","Zamenhof's personal experiences with ethnic conflict in his native Poland motivated his creation of a language promoting peace and understanding."],"effects":["Esperanto became one of the most widely spoken constructed languages, with millions of speakers globally.","The establishment of the Universal Esperanto Association in 1908 solidified the organizational structure for Esperanto advocacy.","The principles outlined in the "Fundamento" influenced subsequent constructed languages and international language movements."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"ludwik-zamenhof","sourceName":"Ludwik Zamenhof","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"fundamento-de-esperanto","targetName":"Fundamento de Esperanto","context":"In 1905, Ludwik Zamenhof published the "Fundamento de Esperanto" as the foundational text for the Esperanto language."},{"sourceSlug":"boulogne-sur-mer","sourceName":"Boulogne-sur-Mer","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"fundamento-de-esperanto","targetName":"Fundamento de Esperanto","context":"The first Universal Esperanto Congress in 1905, held in Boulogne-sur-Mer, was where the "Fundamento de Esperanto" was declared as the authoritative text."},{"sourceSlug":"esperanto","sourceName":"Esperanto","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"fundamento-de-esperanto","targetName":"Fundamento de Esperanto","context":"The "Fundamento de Esperanto" defines the grammatical structure and vocabulary that are essential to the Esperanto language."},{"sourceSlug":"universal-esperanto-association","sourceName":"Universal Esperanto Association","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"fundamento-de-esperanto","targetName":"Fundamento de Esperanto","context":"The Universal Esperanto Association, established in 1908, succeeded in promoting and organizing the community that emerged from the ideas in the "Fundamento de Esperanto.""},{"sourceSlug":"linguistic-movements","sourceName":"Linguistic Movements","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"fundamento-de-esperanto","targetName":"Fundamento de Esperanto","context":"The principles of inclusivity and neutrality in the "Fundamento de Esperanto" influenced later linguistic movements advocating for constructed languages."}],"places":[{"name":"Boulogne-sur-Mer, France","role":"Site of Declaration"},{"name":"Warsaw, Poland","role":"Birthplace of Zamenhof"},{"name":"Berlin, Germany","role":"Major Esperanto Center"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Fundamento de Esperanto","type":"Reference article","slug":"fundamento-de-esperanto"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q427827","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fundamento_de_Esperanto"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Fundamento de Esperanto contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Fundamento de Esperanto (Foundation of Esperanto) is the normative reference work of the Esperanto language, published in 1905 by the language's creator, Ludwig Lazarus Zamenhof (1859–1917), a Polish Jewish ophthalmologist from Białystok — and accepted by the First World Congress of Esperanto (Boulogne-sur-Mer, 1905) as the inviolable basis of the language. The Fundamento consists of three parts: the Antaŭparolo (preface), the Gramatiko (a 16-rule grammar that defines the morphological structure of Esperanto in its entirety), and the Ekzercaro (a set of exercises demonstrating the language in use), along with a vocabulary of approximately 900 root words in multiple languages. Zamenhof's preface declares that the Fundamento is to be considered the immutable foundation of Esperanto — no authority, including Zamenhof himself, may alter it — a declaration that established the principle of democratic linguistic governance that distinguishes Esperanto from other constructed languages.

Esperanto was first published in 1887 in Zamenhof's Unua Libro ('First Book'), but the Fundamento of 1905 is the definitive normative text that fixed the language's grammar and core vocabulary. Zamenhof designed Esperanto as a politically neutral international auxiliary language that would enable communication across linguistic, national, and ethnic barriers — his Białystok childhood, in a city divided between Russians, Poles, Germans, and Jews with no common language, was the direct inspiration for the project. The 16 grammatical rules of the Fundamento — which cover all the morphological regularities of Esperanto in less than a page — are designed to be learnable in hours, in contrast to the years required for natural languages.

Esperanto is the most successful constructed language in history — with estimates of 1–2 million speakers worldwide, a rich literary and cultural tradition, UNESCO recognition, and a global community of native speakers (the Denaskuloj, children raised with Esperanto as a first language). The Fundamento remains the normative basis of the language after 120 years, and Zamenhof's vision of a politically neutral international auxiliary language as the foundation of international communication has influenced all subsequent thinking about language planning and linguistic human rights.","subjects":["Constructed Language","Modern Era","Zamenhof","Esperanto","Language Planning","International Communication","Jewish History","Linguistics"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The "Fundamento de Esperanto" significantly shaped international communication efforts, influencing over 2 million speakers today and establishing a structured community around a constructed language.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
